 ## Overview
-Uniform Volume Ray Marching is a rendering technique for participating media with constant opacity and density, using ray marching to estimate the integral of light scattering [S1, unofficial]. It assumes a single light source and constant volume properties, allowing precomputation of attenuation factors [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its sibling, [Main Ray Marching with Frostbite Integration](4931-main-ray-marching-with-frostbite-integration.md), by its strict assumption of uniform volume properties, which simplifies the transmission calculation to a constant per-step factor [S1, unofficial].
+Uniform Volume Ray Marching is a rendering technique for participating media with constant opacity and density, using ray marching to estimate the integral of light scattering [S1, unofficial]. It assumes a single light source and constant volume properties, allowing precomputation of attenuation factors [S1, unofficial]. Forward ray marching is preferred over backward ray marching for uniform volumes because it allows for early termination when transmission becomes very small, optimizing performance [S1, unofficial].
 
